  [PREFLIGHT] MSL port 'msl_0': the board this run solves is not the board you declared — the substrate under the port realizes 985.0µm against a declared h_sub=787.0µm (+25.2%; read off the run grid's assembled permittivity under the port (5 cell(s)); sim.fidelity_report() reports the same number). The half-open rasterizer rule (rfx/geometry/csg.py) rounds a substrate face that is not on a mesh node UP to the next cell. This is a DIFFERENT PHYSICAL BOARD, not an extraction error: Hammerstad-Jensen Z0 moves about 0.60% per 1% of substrate thickness (measured on this repo's own scripts/diagnostics/msl_z0_bias_floor_sweep/msl_z0_bias_floor_sweep_realized_anchor.json, row 'misaligned 60um' — the one row whose realized trace width equals the declared 600.0µm, so h is the only variable: h 254.0→300.0µm, +18.1%, moves Z0_HJ 47.895→53.106Ω, +10.9%), so this board is worth roughly 15% in Z0 versus the one you specified. This advisory's 8.3% threshold is that sensitivity carried back from check 2's own <5% Z0-bias contract: 5%/0.601 = 8.3%; it is NOT a claim about extractor bias (scored against the board it actually solves, the extractor tracks Hammerstad-Jensen to within 0.4%). Choose dx so h_sub/dx is an integer (e.g. dx = 157.4µm = h_sub/5), or place a mesh node at h_sub=787.0µm on a non-uniform profile; or accept the realized board and quote its 985.0µm thickness rather than the declared one.
